hello and welcome to this talk from the  Yale Child Study Center program for  anxiety disorders I'm Ella Leibovitz and  in today's talk we'll look at what  anxiety is and how it commonly manifests  in children and adolescents we'll talk  about the common anxiety disorders and  how they're diagnosed and we'll discuss  just how prevalent these problems are in  the pediatric population in some  additional talks will discuss cognitive  behavioral therapy or CBT for childhood  anxiety disorders and look at keys to  successfully implementing CBT with  children suffering from an anxiety  disorder for example we'll take a look  at how to successfully plan and execute  exposures with a child suffering from  anxiety in another talk we'll discuss  the role that anxiety can play in the  family and the effect that it has on  parents and we'll talk about strategies  for parents to successfully support and  parent the child suffering from an  anxiety disorder so what is anxiety well  the term is used in many ways but  overall it refers to the response of an  individual to any situation or stimulus  that they perceive as a threat to  themselves there are four major  components to the anxious response the  physiological emotional cognitive and  behavioral aspects of anxiety and all of  these are likely to come into play when  a child is experiencing an anxious  response the emotion most commonly  associated with anxiety is of course  that of fear in fact fear is often used  as a synonym for anxiety but anxiety can  also impact other emotions a child  suffering from anxiety is also likely to  feel more anger and irritability perhaps  as a manifestation of the fite element  in flight-or-fight  additionally anxiety can impede the  experience and the expression of  positive emotions such as happiness or  enthusiasm and this contributes to  anxiety causing an emotion of the  emotional life of a child being rather  bleak some of them were dramatic  manifestations of anxiety are associated  with the physiological symptoms that a  child experiences when undergoing  anxiety children experiencing anxiety  will display both acute short-term  physiological signs of arousal such as  increased heart rate rapid breathing  dizziness and lightheadedness but  they'll also likely experience and  display more chronic and ongoing signs  of stress and anxiety such as recurrent  abdominal pain headaches and various  impacts of loss of sleep another effect  that anxiety has on an individual is in  shaping thought patterns and this is the  cognitive aspect of anxiety children  experiencing anxiety will likely have  many scarier scenarios running through  their minds and they're likely to  interpret even relatively neutral cues  as signaling threat children who are  experiencing anxiety are also likely to  display a kind of tunnel vision that  keeps them narrowly focused on whatever  it is that trigger triggering the  anxiety this isn't an accident but  rather it's a healthy and adaptive  aspect of the anxious response that's  useful when the trigger for the anxiety  is a real threat for example if I were  walking home from work one day I might  have any number of thoughts running  through my mind I might be thinking  about something I have to do later today  work I have to catch up on or maybe a  friend they'd like to see or even just  what I want to have for supper but if I  were to suddenly notice a truck about to  run me over all of those thoughts would  probably be vanished from my mind and  I'd be focused very narrowly on the  immediate threat of the truck and of  course this is a helpful and adaptive  response because I'm unlikely to do any  of those other things if I've been  smushed by a truck but for a child  suffering from an anxiety disorder the  world might seem full of trucks  constantly bearing down on them causing  them to experience anxiety all the time  and causing great interference to their  ability to focus their attention on any  of the other aspects of development such  as social life or school work another  cognitive effect of anxiety is the  tendency of children experience  an anxiety disorder to greatly  overestimate the probability of risk  primarily children will exaggerate the  likelihood of the situations that make  them anxious and that are the source of  the anxiety disorder so for example a  child with a fear of going to sleep at  night because of burglars the child who  thinks that burglars may come to the  house might estimate that the  probability of that happening is maybe  around 50% now obviously that's grossly  exaggerated because if that were the  case we'd expect the house to get broken  into every other night and of course  that doesn't happen but anxiety will  cause the child to overestimate the risk  in fact not only will the child  overestimate the risk that's the source  of the anxiety disorder but when anxious  children will also overestimate any risk  and will consider all negative events to  be more likely so that same child who  thinks that it's likely a burglar will  come to the house that night is also  likely to think that their that they'll  do badly on a test the next day or that  they'll get into some other negative  event again this can contribute to the  overall distress and impairment that's  caused by a chronic anxiety disorder in  a child the third aspect of anxiety is  that of behavior or rather the fourth  aspect of that of behavior and the  effect that anxiety has when a child's  behavioral patterns first and foremost  anxiety causes avoidance in other words  staying away from those situations that  trigger the anxiety again this is a  healthy and adaptive aspect of the  anxious response to keep us away from  things that pose risk but when a child  has an anxiety disorder and perceives  situations that are realistically not  harmful as being threatening avoidance  means that they don't have the  opportunity to learn that those  situations are actually quite safe so  for example a child who has a fear of  dogs will probably avoid any contact  with dogs and will be robbed if the  Opera  unity to learn that most dogs are  actually quite friendly or a little girl  who has a fear of elevators and walks up  the stairs might say to herself I'm safe  because I took the stairs but won't have  the opportunity to learn that she could  have been equally safe had she taken the  elevator sometimes avoidance seems like  the only way to lower anxiety once it's  already been triggered so for example a  child with persistent worry might engage  in a special kind of avoidance in  reassurance seeking a child might pass  their parents with never-ending  questions about their worries in order  to lower the anxiety that's triggered by  those thoughts when this happens the  child won't learn that if they don't  engage in that kind of avoidance anxiety  is likely to go away on its own learning  that lesson that anxiety will go away if  the child doesn't engage in avoidance is  a central part of cognitive behavioral  therapy for anxiety disorders but what  are some of the anxiety disorders that  are commonly diagnosed in children and  in adolescence specific phobias refer to  exaggerated and irrational fear of any  particular situation or stimulus these  commonly include fears of animals such  as dogs or snakes they can include other  situations like Heights or water or  darkness or they could include a fear of  needles or just about any other stimulus  can be the focus of a child's phobia  sometimes the phobia can easily be  related to a specific trigger in the  child's history such as the child who  has a fear of dogs and can date that to  the day that a dog bit them but in many  other cases there really isn't a clear  trigger for the phobia and it's usually  not helpful to invest too much energy in  treatment and trying to identify the  trigger that might have been the onset  of a child's phobia one special kind of  phobia is termed separation anxiety  disorder and this describes the child  who's filled with fear that's  operation from caretakers usually  parents children suffering from  separation anxiety disorder can become  very anxious even at very brief  separations such as being in another  room from their parents for a few  minutes a child with separation anxiety  is likely to imagine all kinds of awful  things that could happen to them when  the parents are away for example being  kidnapped or getting hurt many children  with separation anxiety disorder will  focus on negative things that could  happen to the parents when they're not  next to them for example getting into a  car accident or just disappearing and  never coming back  one other phobia is called social phobia  for sometimes social anxiety disorder  and this refers to a kind of extreme and  painful shyness that can cause a child  to avoid social interaction the child  with social phobia will usually imagine  negative outcomes of social interaction  such as making a fool of themselves by  saying something silly or making a  mistake and being laughed at sometimes  the avoidance will focus on specific  kinds of social interaction such as  speaking in front of class or talking on  the phone but sometimes it will be a  much broader array of interaction that  the child avoids leading to almost  complete self isolation and although  children will expend tremendous amounts  of effort in ensuring this isolation the  child with social anxiety disorder  doesn't necessarily suffer from a lack  of social awareness were even from a  real disinterest in social interaction  in fact many children with social phobia  actually fervently wish for friendship  and company even as they avoid social  interaction because of their fear a  feeling bad if they were to engage with  another person G a D or generalized  anxiety disorder could be characterized  as the child who has a kind of giant  antenna on their head finely tuned to  pick up just anything there is to worry  about children with generalized anxiety  disorder will usually move from one  worry to another but never feel relaxed  and  one day it might be an item on the news  that the child saw saying that  cellphones might cause cancer and then  the child could worry for weeks about  all the people in their family that used  cell phones and could get sick and died  another time the child might overhear  parents discussing the family's finances  and become tremendously worried that  they're all going to be homeless or if  they won't have enough money a child  with generalized anxiety disorder might  stay up all night doing and redoing  homework because it just doesn't seem  good enough and they're worried that  they won't do well in school and about  the outcomes that would have for their  life children with generalized anxiety  disorder will often engage in a lot of  reassurance seeking behavior and ask  their parents never-ending questions in  order to alleviate the distress caused  by the worry they'll often manifest a  lot of chronic physical symptoms such as  stomachache and backache and they'll  often have sleep disturbances as well  all of these symptoms together lead to  great overlap between generalized  anxiety disorder and symptoms of  depression one special kind of worry is  the kind diagnosed in OCD or obsessive  compulsive disorder children with  obsessive-compulsive disorder experience  a kind of intrusive thoughts worries  that come into their mind unwanted and  that they wish they could stop thinking  about but they just can't and they  usually have to perform special rituals  or repeated behaviors in order to  alleviate the distress caused by those  worries typical obsessions include  things like thinking about germs  contamination and sickness or thinking  about  awful things that could happen or even  terrible things that the child fears  they might do or already have done such  as hurting someone or even killing  someone parents are often tremendously  distressed that hearing that their child  has these thoughts but it's important to  keep in mind that having an obsession or  worry doesn't mean that the child  secretly wants to do those awful things  or even that they're particularly likely  to do them in fact it really just  reflects the child's desire to be good  and  not to do bad things and the fear that  they lose control over themselves  typical compulsions include things like  repeated checking behaviors such as  checking that the doors are locked or  the windows are shut  they might include washing rituals and  sometimes children will have to do  things in a specific number of times  such as tapping the table three times or  turning the lights on an or four times  many children also have compulsions that  relate to symmetry such as the need to  line things up on a shelf just so or  even to even out the two sides of the  body by touching with their left hand  something something that they touch with  their right in order to ensure a kind of  inner symmetry another anxiety disorder  in children and in adolescents as well  as adults because panic disorder panic  disorder is characterized by recurrent  panic attacks which are a horrible  experience in which it's as if a wire  trips in the child's brain and they're  just awash in tremendous waves of  anxiety with dramatic physical arousal  heart racing chest pain and rapid  breathing and usually accompanied by  dreadful cognitions such as the thought  this is the end I'm dying or I'm losing  my mind and going insane although panic  attacks will almost always subside  within around 15 or 20 minutes many  children will come away from the  experience with one thought which is I'm  never going to experience that again and  this can lead to symptoms of agoraphobia  or the avoidance of situations because  of fear of panic symptoms taken together  the anxiety disorders are probably the  most common disorders of childhood and  adolescence and probably of adulthood as  well studies have estimated that up to  one in three individuals is likely to  suffer from an anxiety disorder at some  point in their lives and most adults  will recollect that their symptoms  actually had their own set in relatively  early childhood maybe as many as 15  percent of children suffer from an  anxiety disorder and in a given time  many of those children however will go  undiagnosed and untreated for a long  time and this is particularly disturbing  given two pieces of information that  have gotten considerable support over  the past years from research into  anxiety we can think of these as the  good news and the bad news of child and  anxiety disorders the bad news about  anxiety is that it doesn't tend to go  away if left untreated of course all  children will experience transient fears  and age-appropriate fears that will come  and go as they develop but when an  anxiety disorder has taken root in a  child's life and has become persistent  source of distress or something that  interferes with the child's ability to  function in an age-appropriate way that  anxiety disorder is unlikely to just  disappear if action isn't taken to help  the child to overcome the fear this is  likely because the ongoing avoidance  ensures that the child never has the  opportunity to learn that their anxiety  is unbaked in reality the good news  about anxiety is that in the multitude  of studies that have looked at treatment  for childhood and adolescent anxiety  disorders including studies of both  medication and cognitive behavioral  therapy most children who participated  in those studies have actually improved  so that they were either cured of their  disorder or experience significant  improvement in their symptoms taken  together the fact that anxiety disorders  are not likely to go away on their own  but will often respond to treatment we  have a powerful argument for diagnosing  and treating anxiety disorders early on  in additional talks we'll look at what  goes in to treating childhood anxiety  disorders what is cognitive behavioral  therapy and how to implement it  successfully and we'll also look at the  role that anxiety plays in the families  and at how parents can successfully help  their children to overcome anxiety  disorders thank you
 

